     "result_text_tail": "he thinly staffed stores.\n4. Give notice on the six leases that expire.\n5. Rebid the delivery contract instead of accepting the proposed 3% increase.\n\n**Choices I made in place of asking you** (all listed on slide 22):\n- **Fiscal-year timing:** I treated FY2026 as the first plan year and the covenant test year, since the brief implies it is still open. If FY2026 has already closed by the February meeting, each phase moves out a year but the order of actions stays the same.\n- **Wage rise:** I applied the 2027 +4% to the whole store payroll, which is cautious.\n- **Promotions:** I assumed 70% of the savings from cutting promotions are kept.\n- **Vendor funding:** I valued each point at $1.13M. The pack doesn't report markdown spend, so I derived it from the promotion figures; this is the least certain number.\n- **Weak stores:** the pack gives only totals for the nine, so I treated them as equal in size.\n- **One-time costs:** only the $7.5M lease-exit cost comes from the pack. The other cost lines (about $12.6M) are my estimates, labelled as such.\n\nI couldn't open the deck in PowerPoint or Keynote from here. I checked every slide for text overflow with my own renderer and found none, but the chart number labels and the dashed target and covenant lines have only been checked by calculation. Look at slides 4, 14 and 16 when you first open it.\n\nFiles are in `deliverable/`:\n- Harlow_margin_plan_board_Feb2027.pptx\n- harlow_margin_model.py \u2014 produces every number in the deck\n- README.md"
    }
